# ── RETIRED MODULES, 2026-10-20 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 01_stat, 02_literature, 03_kraken and 04_kallisto were moved to superseded/ and untracked
# (709 files, 74 MB of which 60 MB was figures). They are OUT of the repo and ON disk, which
# is the split Leon asked for: the history keeps every version, the working tree stops
# carrying four retired pipelines next to the two live ones.
#
# What replaced each, and which of their outputs are still read, is recorded in SUPERSEDED.md,
# which IS tracked. Read that before reaching into this directory.
#
# One rule, by directory, rather than the sixteen per-file blocks that used to live here.
# Those blocks had already failed in both directions, which is the argument for a directory
# rule and is the same argument 01_literature and 02_classify's data/ rules won on.
/superseded/

# Log history dirs + .latest symlinks are now TRACKED (transparency — Leon's
# call 2026-09-01: full run history should be visible in the repo, not just
# code). Do not re-add exclusions for logs/history/ or logs/*.latest here.

# Stray BUSCO container job logs that end up at repo root on Setonix
/busco_*.log
busco_downloads/

# Old output/ directory — local pipeline data, all superseded by module layout
/output/

# Claude Code project settings
.claude/
CLAUDE.md

# Colleague correspondence / external documents
kristina/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.env

# SLURM .out/.err: TRACKED inside a module's slurm/logs/, ignored at the repo root.
#
# Leon's call 2026-10-20, on the same transparency grounds as logs/history/: a job's .out is the
# only record of what a run actually did, and it is written on a filesystem under a 21-day
# purge. Measured when the rule was made: of 197 jobs sacct still remembered, 92 had no log on
# either machine. Most were tests and cancelled attempts, but kraken_build COMPLETED x3 and
# kraken_annotate COMPLETED x4 were among them, and those are gone for good.
#
# They are small. 02_classify/slurm/logs is 402 files in 1.2 MB, largest 40 KB. If one ever is
# not small, that is a job printing in a loop and the fix is the job (see the 13.8 MB
# acacia_dbv3 .err, which retried a hard S3 refusal in a tight loop).
#
# Do NOT add an exclusion for */slurm/logs/*.out or *.err here. The rule below is scoped to the
# REPO ROOT with a leading slash, and it exists only because older scripts dropped logs there
# before every --output path pointed at a module's own slurm/logs/.
/*.out
/*.err

# Personal reference/prose: pipeline schematic, literature notes, manuscript drafts.
# Kept on disk for navigation, not version-controlled (Leon's call 2026-09-24).
# docs/ holds drafts, the manuscript and collaborators' material: all local.
# The pipeline schematic is the exception - it is documentation a reader of the repo
# needs, so it is tracked.
docs/*
!docs/pipeline_schematic.md

# 01_literature: search returns and resolved accession lists. Data, per the code-not-data
# policy; the prompts that produced them are tracked and are the reproducible part.
# Everything DERIVED lives under data/, one directory per medallion layer. The exception is
# gold/: generated, but a few kilobytes of summary numbers worth having in history. git does
# not descend into an excluded directory, so re-including it needs the directory itself
# re-included first.
01_literature/data/**
!01_literature/data/gold/
!01_literature/data/gold/*.tsv

# The hand-built records used to live here as exceptions to the rule above, which meant
# tracking depended on whether somebody remembered `git add -f`: joins.tsv was never added to
# the list and three generated tables were force-added by mistake. They now live in
# 01_literature/seeds/, which is OUTSIDE this rule and therefore tracked by default, and
# 01_literature/data/gold/ holds the small human-readable outputs. See
# 01_literature/_layout.py (named _layout, not _paths: the repo root owns _paths.py and a
# second module of that name shadows it).
#
#   seeds/        AUTHORED, irreplaceable, tracked. Losing it costs re-reading every paper.
#                 It stays OUT of data/ deliberately: data/ means "delete it and it rebuilds",
#                 and these are the only files in the module for which that is false.
#   data/bronze/  immutable external captures (runinfo, BioSample attrs, GEO, ENA), ignored.
#   data/silver/  generated tables, ignored. Losing it costs a refetch and nothing else.
#   data/gold/    generated but tiny and tracked: summary numbers over the project's life.

# 01_literature working files. studies/ is the manual drop zone and holds publisher PDFs and
# supplementary spreadsheets, which are not ours to redistribute (same grounds as
# 02_literature/02_text/data/manual_pdfs/). The adapters ARE the reproducible part and are
# tracked; so are the Undermind reports and the prompts.
# git does not descend into an excluded directory, so re-including a file inside one needs the
# directories re-included at every level first. `studies/*` alone would hide the adapters.
01_literature/studies/**
!01_literature/studies/*/
!01_literature/studies/*/adapter.yaml
# SOURCE.txt is the per-study provenance note: the citation, where the text came from, which
# accessions it sources and any warning about the study. Re-included BY RULE because tracking
# it by `git add -f` habit had already lost 10 of 33 of them, which is the same failure the
# seeds/ split was made to end (see 01_literature/_layout.py).
!01_literature/studies/*/SOURCE*.txt
01_literature/*.pdf
01_literature/*.xls
01_literature/*.xlsx

# WSL writes an alternate-data-stream sidecar next to anything downloaded through Windows.
# Pure noise, never ours, and they appear beside every PDF and spreadsheet dropped into
# 01_literature/studies/.
*:Zone.Identifier

# seeds/ annotation cache: one line per genus from the datasets CLI, purely a speed-up
# for re-running build_seeds.py --annotate. Regenerated, never the record.
02_classify/seeds/.annotation_cache.jsonl

# Own-submission metadata: BioSample attribute templates for OUR OWN unpublished deposits
# (PRJNA1509083 P. nodorum infection timecourse; oat rust field collections with GPS). This is
# unpublished research data for other chapters that was dropped at this repo root, and the repo
# is PUBLIC. Never commit it: move it to the project it belongs to.
/_selfdata/

# 02_classify (2026-10-15), which replaces 03_kraken. Same rule as 01_literature, and for the
# same reason: 03_kraken gave each numbered step its own data/, logs/ and figures/, so tracking
# depended on a per-file negation list that had already failed in both directions (three
# generated tables force-added, ensembl_species.json tracked by accident, and five Setonix-only
# outputs with no rule at all until the renumbering surfaced them).
#
# The step directories here hold .py and nothing else. Everything derived is a medallion layer
# under data/ and is ignored by directory, not by name:
#
#   seeds/        AUTHORED, irreplaceable, tracked by default because it is OUTSIDE data/.
#                 Losing it costs re-deciding what belongs in the database. Its hidden
#                 .annotation_cache.jsonl is a datasets-CLI speed-up and is ignored above.
#   data/bronze/  immutable external captures: the CDS tree, downloaded reads, raw Kraken2
#                 reports, NCBI assembly summaries, BUSCO lineages. ~200 GB, lives on Setonix
#                 scratch; a refetch is the only cost.
#   data/silver/  generated tables: ref_candidates, db_inspect, run_list, host_calls,
#                 _classified. Rebuildable from bronze + seeds.
#   data/gold/    generated but a few kilobytes of summary numbers, TRACKED so the headline
#                 rates keep a history. git does not descend into an excluded directory, so
#                 re-including a file inside one needs the directory itself re-included first.
#
# See 02_classify/_layout.py (named _layout, not _paths: the repo root owns _paths.py and a
# second module of that name shadows it).
02_classify/data/**
!02_classify/data/gold/
!02_classify/data/gold/*.tsv
!02_classify/data/gold/.gitkeep

# SILVER TABLES ARE TRACKED, from 2026-10-19. The layer boundary turned out to be the wrong
# place to cut, because the stated reason for ignoring silver was REBUILDABILITY and gold is
# just as generated. Measured, the real axis is size: silver's twelve tables are 6.6 MB in
# total (largest assemblies.tsv at 2.6 MB) while `silver/db/` alone is 139 GB. Tables and
# artefacts, not layers.
#
# Three things this buys, the first of which is the one that forced it:
#   * run_list.tsv and fetch_plan.tsv have to exist on BOTH machines, and a gitignored silver
#     table has now failed to reach Setonix twice (predict_sample.tsv, ref_candidates.tsv),
#     each time as a job that started and then died on a missing input.
#   * db_inspect.tsv is the coverage denominator for every 05_detect figure, so tracking it
#     means a figure can be rebuilt locally against db_v4 without an scp first.
#   * a diff on ref_candidates.tsv or cds_manifest.tsv is how a database change becomes
#     reviewable, which is the same argument gold already won.
#
# `*.tsv` is deliberately a SINGLE star: it matches tables sitting directly in silver/ and can
# never descend into silver/db/, so the 139 GB index cannot leak in by accident.
!02_classify/data/silver/
!02_classify/data/silver/*.tsv

# ...with one exception, on the same reasoning as the PNG rule below. _classified.tsv is one
# row per candidate detection: 2.4 MB under db_v3, larger under db_v4, and REWRITTEN on every
# threshold sweep. The co-infection rate is threshold-dependent and gets swept often, so this
# is the one silver table that would be re-committed dozens of times in an afternoon. Its
# summary lands in gold, which is where the history belongs.
02_classify/data/silver/_classified.tsv

# 02_classify figures: the PDF is tracked and the PNG is not. The PDF is 132 KB of vector
# paths, is what goes into Illustrator, and regenerates the PNG at any resolution; the PNG is
# 3 MB and had already been re-committed nine times during one afternoon of tuning. The repo
# policy is code, small figures and manifests, and a 3 MB raster rewritten on every parameter
# tweak is not a small figure. Regenerate with db_contents.R.
02_classify/report/figures/*.png

# R writes this whenever a script plots without opening a device explicitly. It is an
# artefact, never an output, and it was committed once by accident.
Rplots.pdf

# Per-run detection images (detect_run_single.R). One PNG per run at ~0.4 MB, so the full
# cohort is hundreds of MB of regenerable figures. The repo holds code, small figures and
# manifests; these are a browsing artefact, rebuilt on demand from detect_runs.tsv.
02_classify/report/figures/runs/
